{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792833371,"nonce":"yMAbZ1Kbq99x8f4/RnMwO1gzaes=","title":"cb_window: the world's loop drives xwindow's external pump","message":"Since xwindow f50e27c and 1415c7c, a host that owns the main thread's loop can drive the window system:\n- `external_pump(true)`: a window's `poll` then only drains queued events;\n- `pump_external(timeout)`: waits for platform events, the timeout or a proxy wake;\n- `external_waker()`: a proxy that interrupts that wait from another thread;\n- `external_pending()`.\n\ncb_window builds these (xwindow 3be8f63) and calls none of them. So a program in `window.wait()` blocks inside winit's pump: the world's other tasks and timers don't run until an event arrives, and a command pushed from another thread doesn't wake it.\n\nThe core now has `sched::host_wait(timeout, wake)` / `host_resumed()`, which Ash's runtime seam slot of the same name uses (hlwindow's HDLL path).\n1. It runs one turn and answers how long the host may wait.\n2. A command pushed meanwhile calls `wake`.\n\nThe integration on caribou's side:\n- With a window open, the world's idle wait (`scheduler_idle`) becomes `host_wait` → `pump_external(timeout)` → `host_resumed`, with `wake` sending the external waker's proxy event.\n- `window.wait()` from a task parks the task on the window's queue instead of pumping.\n\nThe same applies to an ashui/ChimpMonk app on the hlwindow HDLL, which reaches it through Ash's `host_wait` slot. That needs checking against multi-window waits (ChimpMonk 59c227a).","files":null}]}